---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro'; import CtaBand from '../../../components/CtaBand.astro'; import '../../../content/_shared.css';
import { lab } from '../../../data/site.js';
const body = {
 'voice-agent-cost': `
<h2>The bill</h2><p>June 2026, two cloud accounts, ~51,400 call-minutes for our AI calling product: <b>₹1,95,017</b>, of which 96% was the live speech-to-speech API. That is <b>₹3.79 per call-minute</b>. It works well. It is also the single line that decides whether a voice agent makes money for a client.</p>
<h2>Where the minute goes</h2><p>Measured against real billing: the live API consumes audio at about 94 tokens a second, and it bills the open microphone — including silence — so audio-in alone is ₹1.62 a minute. Context is re-billed every turn (₹0.95), audio-out is ₹1.09, post-call processing ₹0.14.</p>
<table><tr><th>Leg</th><th>Live API (measured)</th><th>Cascade (list prices)</th></tr><tr><td>Caller audio in</td><td>₹1.62 — open mic, silence billed</td><td>₹0.05–0.10 — 8.4 s of speech per minute, transcribed per utterance</td></tr><tr><td>Reasoning</td><td>₹0.95 — context re-billed per turn</td><td>₹0.35 → ₹0.15–0.25 with caching</td></tr><tr><td>Agent audio out</td><td>₹1.09</td><td>₹0.22 — 10 s of speech per minute at 25 tokens/s</td></tr><tr><td>Post-call</td><td>₹0.14</td><td>₹0.14</td></tr><tr><td><b>Per call-minute</b></td><td><b>₹3.79</b></td><td><b>≈₹0.76–0.81</b></td></tr></table>
<h2>The cascade</h2><p>Telephony stream ⇄ our orchestrator: voice-activity detection → speech-to-text per utterance → a fast text model with function calling → text-to-speech per sentence → back to the line. Two measured facts made it work: the agent speaks only ~10 seconds per call-minute and the caller ~8.4, so paying per second of <i>speech</i> instead of per second of <i>open line</i> is most of the saving. The text-to-speech leg was measured at exactly 25 output tokens per second of audio (42.6 s of Hindi = 1,064 tokens).</p>
<h2>What we didn't do</h2><p>The original plan was self-hosted speech and language models on GPUs. It was killed by a benchmark — see the bake-off note. No GPUs, linear scaling, no idle burn; self-hosting stays a phase-two option at high volume only.</p>
<div class="tldr"><b>If you're buying:</b> ask any voice-agent vendor for cost per call-minute with silence included. If they quote per API call or "per conversation", they haven't measured it. Ours is ₹1–4 a minute all-in depending on volume and language, and we show it before go-live.</div>`,
 'asr-bake-off': `
<h2>The question</h2><p>Before moving a production voice agent off a live speech API, we needed to know which speech-to-text model actually understands a code-mixed, Punjabi-accented caller on an 8 kHz phone line. Not on a benchmark set — on our calls.</p>
<h2>The corpus</h2><p>160 recordings pulled from the telephony provider, 125 of them genuine conversations (209 minutes), 93 in Hindi and 29 in English, heavily code-mixed, from an education campaign in Bathinda. Mono MP3 at 8 kHz — the real thing, not studio audio.</p>
<h2>Why word-error rate lied</h2><p>A strong self-hosted model came in at roughly 45% "WER". Most of that was orthography: it wrote Hindi words in Roman script where the reference used Devanagari, and vice versa. Meaning-level accuracy was far better than the number suggested — and worse than a hosted model in the places that mattered.</p>
<h2>The method that worked</h2><p>Diarised verbatim references were produced by a large model and checked; then a second model acted as a <b>judge</b>, scoring only meaning-level damage: would an agent reading this transcript misunderstand the caller? Entity errors — names, numbers, dates — were counted separately because they are what break a booking. On that scoring the hosted, cascaded models won, and the GPU plan was cancelled on evidence rather than preference.</p>
<h2>What transferred</h2><p>The judge harness became the way we evaluate every speech change now: real calls, meaning-level scoring, entity errors counted. It is also how we tune per-client: 200 of your calls, judged the same way, before the agent goes live.</p>
<div class="tldr"><b>If you're buying:</b> ask to hear the agent on <i>your</i> callers, not a demo script, and ask how accuracy was measured. "95% accuracy" without a corpus and a scoring rule means nothing on an Indian phone line.</div>`,
 'two-rail-learning-app': `
<h2>The product</h2><p>A voice-first English teacher for students in tier-2 and tier-3 India: the learner speaks, an AI teacher corrects kindly, every mistake is remembered and the next session is built around it. Sold at about ₹299 a month. The economics decide whether the product can exist.</p>
<h2>The observation</h2><p>Roughly 80% of a learner's minutes are <b>practice</b> — repeat-after-me, read-aloud, pronunciation drills. The target sentence is known in advance. No open-ended conversation is needed, so nothing needs a server: scoring runs on the phone at near-zero marginal cost.</p>
<h2>Two rails</h2><table><tr><th>Rail</th><th>Share of minutes</th><th>Where it runs</th><th>Marginal cost</th></tr><tr><td>Practice — known target sentence</td><td>~80%</td><td>On the phone</td><td>Near zero</td></tr><tr><td>Conversation — open-ended talk</td><td>~20%</td><td>Server: speech-to-text → small teacher model → voice reply</td><td>Per minute of talk</td></tr></table><p>At 15 minutes of use a day, only three of them touch a server. That is what makes ₹299 a month a real price rather than a loss-leader — and it is the single design decision the concept deck was built around.</p>
<h2>What this generalises to</h2><p>Most "AI features" have a practice rail hiding inside them: the part where the answer is already known and the model is just checking. Find it, run it cheap, and spend the expensive model only where the conversation is genuinely open.</p>
<div class="tldr"><b>If you're buying an app with AI in it:</b> ask what the AI costs per user per month at your price point. If nobody has split the cheap rail from the expensive one, the unit economics haven't been done.</div>`,
 'ai-is-the-project-manager': `
<h2>The floor</h2><p>A garment sourcing business in Tiruppur: 200+ live orders for European buyers, a dozen factories, time-and-action plans in Excel one file per order, and chasing as somebody's full-time job. The build wasn't the hard part; deciding what the process should be was.</p>
<h2>Where the AI sits</h2><p>Not in a chat box in the corner. It is the project manager:</p><ul><li><b>Orchestrates the plan.</b> Every order gets a time-and-action schedule at creation, back-scheduled from the ship date across 28 stages, and re-planned when a date slips.</li><li><b>Creates and assigns the work.</b> Tasks appear for the right person at the right stage without a manager writing them — for every one of 200+ orders.</li><li><b>Reads the inputs.</b> Status is pulled out of email and three buyer portals, so the system updates itself instead of waiting for someone to remember.</li><li><b>Flags what matters today.</b> A short list each morning of the things that genuinely need a human decision.</li></ul>
<h2>The numbers</h2><p>112 AI suggestions raised to the team; 196 exceptions flagged rather than hunted; seven spreadsheets retired; a $2.18M order book visible by value and stage for the first time. And one deliberate design choice that looks like a bug: a skipped stage stays <b>overdue</b> until someone logs it, because skipped stages are how orders go quiet.</p>
<h2>What went wrong, and what it taught</h2><p>An outage in a model provider's key once silently dropped 37% of inbound emails for weeks while every health check stayed green. The lesson went into every build since: a deterministic sweep is the floor under any AI extraction, and silence is a failure mode you have to alarm on explicitly.</p>
<div class="tldr"><b>If you're buying:</b> ask where the AI sits in the workflow. If the answer is "there's a chat assistant", the process is still being run by people remembering things.</div>`,
};
export function getStaticPaths() { return lab.map(n => ({ params: { slug: n.slug }, props: { n } })); }
const { n } = Astro.props; const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const schema = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article', headline: n.title, datePublished: n.date, author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'SparkFuse Business Solutions LLP' } };
---
<Base title={`${n.title} | SparkFuse Lab`} description={n.tldr.slice(0, 155)} section="lab" schema={schema}>
  <div class="hero"><div class="mono">Lab · {n.date}</div><h1 class="h1" style="font-size: 40px;">{n.title}</h1><div class="tldr">{n.tldr}</div></div>
  <div style="padding: 24px var(--gx) 0;"><div class="prose" set:html={body[n.slug]} /></div>
  <div class="sec"><div class="card q" style="flex-direction: row; gap: 20px; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap;"><div class="mono">Related</div><a href={`${base}/products/ai-calling-agent/`} style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 13px;">AI calling agent →</a><a href={`${base}/solutions/custom-ai-models/`} style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 13px;">Custom AI models →</a><a href={`${base}/work/`} style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 13px;">Work →</a></div></div>
  <CtaBand />
</Base>
